<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cilicia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cilicia is a historical region on the southeastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. It controlled routes between Anatolia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> through the Cilician Gates. After 129 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> was reduced to a Syrian rump state controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, northern Syria and Cilicia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cilicia.jpg" alt="Cilicia" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Cilicia_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Armenian Kingdom of <b>Cilicia</b> was built by refugees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For a brief period Tigranes the Great raised Armenia into an empire that ended the Seleucids and ruled from Iberia and Albania through Cappadocia, <b>Cilicia</b>, Armenian Mesopotamia, Osroene, Adiabene, Syria, Assyria, Commagene and Sophene to Judea and Atropatene.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Wealthy merchants such as Urtenu and Rapanu kept their own archives in large town houses, and Hittite edicts regulated the activities of traders from Ura in <b>Cilicia</b> who worked in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Samos had its own sanctuary of Hera at Naucratis in Egypt, and it founded colonies such as Perinthus on the Propontis, Nagidus and Celenderis in <b>Cilicia</b> and Minoa on Amorgos, while exiles from the island settled Dicaearchia, the later Puteoli, in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>What remained after 129 BCE was a Syrian rump state centred on Antioch and extending into <b>Cilicia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Plutarch wrote that the pirates of <b>Cilicia</b> celebrated secret rites of Mithras, and Ulansey dates the first evidence to the middle of the 1st century BCE; Manfred Clauss holds that the mysteries were not practised until the 1st century CE, and C. M. Daniels finds the link unclear.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
